---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Security policy and vulnerability reporting"
  description="The mod_pagespeed security policy: how to report a vulnerability, the threat model, supply chain security and safe harbor for researchers."
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16">
    <h1 class="h1-hero text-text-body">Security policy</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-text-muted">
      mod_pagespeed processes untrusted content &mdash; HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and images from
      origin servers. We take this seriously.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-12 space-y-10">
      <!-- Reporting vulnerabilities -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Reporting vulnerabilities</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          If you've found a security vulnerability, please email
          <a
            href="mailto:security@modpagespeed.com"
            class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
          >
            security@modpagespeed.com
          </a>
          with details, or use GitHub private vulnerability reporting on the
          <a
            href="https://github.com/We-Amp/mod_pagespeed/security/advisories/new"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
          >
            module
          </a>{' '}
          or the
          <a
            href="https://github.com/We-Amp/pagespeed-optimizer/security/advisories/new"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
          >
            optimizer worker
          </a>{' '}
          repository. Do not open a public GitHub issue for security vulnerabilities.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          We do not publish a PGP key for reports. GitHub private vulnerability reporting is the
          encrypted channel; if you need another one, ask by email and we will arrange it before you
          send details.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- End-of-life notices -->
      <div>
        <h2 id="end-of-life-notices" class="h2-section text-text-body">
          If we emailed you about an end-of-life release
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          We write, once, to an organization whose public website answers with an
          <code>X-Mod-Pagespeed</code> or <code>X-Page-Speed</code> response header identifying a
          PageSpeed release that no longer receives security fixes. A notice from us looks like
          this:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-text-muted">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-text-annotation font-bold">&bull;</span>
            <span>
              By email it comes from <code>otto@modpagespeed.com</code>, DKIM-signed for
              modpagespeed.com, as plain text without attachments, to an address published for
              security reports, normally your organization's own.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-text-annotation font-bold">&bull;</span>
            <span>
              It names the host and the header, so you can check the claim yourself, and it lists
              the requests we made to check it.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-text-annotation font-bold">&bull;</span>
            <span>
              It is sent once. It makes no offer and asks for no payment or bounty; one sentence
              states that we earn our income from support and consulting. We do not write again
              unless you reply.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-text-annotation font-bold">&bull;</span>
            <span>
              To confirm a notice, forward it to
              <a
                href="mailto:security@modpagespeed.com"
                class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
              >
                security@modpagespeed.com
              </a>
              and we will tell you whether we sent it.
            </span>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          Before writing we request the homepage (once with the documented
          <code>?PageSpeed=off</code> parameter), one static file linked from it, and
          <code>security.txt</code>, following redirects, and the homepage once more just before
          sending. We request the homepage again after 30 days and after 90 days, to see whether the
          header has changed. These requests carry the User-Agent
          <code class="break-all">weamp-notice-check/1 (+https://modpagespeed.com/security/)</code>.
          If a site refuses our checker, we look at its home page once in an ordinary browser, and
          later follow-ups use a public web crawl archive rather than a request from us. The notice
          lists every request we made, including any others to public pages. We do not log in, scan
          or test for vulnerabilities, and we do not publish host names.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          If you would rather not hear from us, reply and say so: we stop the follow-up requests too
          and keep your domain on a do-not-contact list. How we handle the correspondence is in the{' '}
          <a
            href="https://we-amp.com/privacy/#end-of-life-notices"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
          >
            privacy policy
          </a>
          .
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- What to include -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">What to include</h2>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-text-muted">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-text-annotation font-bold">&bull;</span>
            <span>A description of the vulnerability and its potential impact.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-text-annotation font-bold">&bull;</span>
            <span>Steps to reproduce the issue.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-text-annotation font-bold">&bull;</span>
            <span>Affected versions, if known.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-text-annotation font-bold">&bull;</span>
            <span>Your assessment of severity and impact.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- Our commitment -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Our commitment</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          We acknowledge reports and follow a coordinated disclosure process: we work on a fix with
          the reporter, and after the fix is released the reporter may publish details. Critical
          vulnerabilities affecting production deployments jump the queue.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          We keep reporters informed as we work on a fix. If a vulnerability is being exploited, we
          do not wait for a scheduled release: the fix is released as soon as it is ready.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- Supported versions -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Supported versions</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          Security fixes are made in the latest release. Upgrading to it is the supported path from
          any earlier release. Security fixes reach every user at the same time, through the same
          public channels: nobody receives a fixed build before the public release.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- Safe harbor -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Safe harbor</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          We will not pursue legal action against security researchers who act in good faith. Good
          faith means: you report the vulnerability to us before disclosing publicly, you don't
          access or modify other users' data, and you don't degrade the service for others.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- Scope -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Scope</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          This security policy covers the mod_pagespeed software (the module, the optimizer worker,
          and the cache layer) on Apache, nginx and IIS, and the
          <a
            href="https://modpagespeed.com"
            class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
          >
            modpagespeed.com
          </a>{' '}
          website.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- Security updates -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Security updates</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          Every security fix is listed on the
          <a
            href="/docs/release-notes/#security-updates"
            class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
          >
            release notes
          </a>
          , with the release that carried it: the impact, the affected and fixed versions, and
          whether to update, plus the CVE id when one has been assigned. The announcements describe
          the impact; they do not describe how to trigger an issue.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- Evidence -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Evidence</h2>
        <div class="mt-4 space-y-6">
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-text-body">Security disclosure</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-text-muted">
              Report vulnerabilities to
              <a
                href="mailto:security@modpagespeed.com"
                class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
              >
                security@modpagespeed.com
              </a>
              . Reports go by email or through GitHub private vulnerability reporting; the
              repositories' SECURITY.md files state the same policy. See
              <a
                href="/.well-known/security.txt"
                class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
              >
                security.txt
              </a>
              .
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-text-body">CVE history</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-text-muted">
              Tracked in the
              <a
                href="/docs/release-notes/#security-updates"
                class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
              >
                release notes
              </a>
              .
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-text-body">Dependency audits</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-text-muted">
              The dependency gate blocks a change on any fixable vulnerability of medium severity or
              higher, checked against a pinned vulnerability database, and a daily scan against the
              live feed catches newly published ones.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-text-body">Continuous AI security scanning</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-text-muted">
              mod_pagespeed has been enrolled in{' '}
              <a
                href="https://github.com/anthropics/oss-scanner/blob/main/projects/mod_pagespeed/project.yaml"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
              >
                Anthropic&rsquo;s OSS Scanner
              </a>{' '}
              since October 2026. Its enrolment config and{' '}
              <a
                href="https://github.com/We-Amp/mod_pagespeed/blob/master/.oss-scanner/threat_model.md"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
              >
                threat model
              </a>{' '}
              are public.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-text-body">Continuous fuzzing</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-text-muted">
              mod_pagespeed has been enrolled in{' '}
              <a
                href="https://github.com/google/oss-fuzz/tree/master/projects/mod_pagespeed"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
              >
                OSS-Fuzz
              </a>
              , Google&rsquo;s fuzzing service for open-source projects, since November 2026. Its
              HTML parser, JavaScript minifier, CSS parser and rule-pack code are fuzzed
              continuously; findings are disclosed under OSS-Fuzz&rsquo;s standard policy, and
              reports reach security@modpagespeed.com.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-text-body">SBOM</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-text-muted">
              An SPDX 2.3 SBOM, with a VEX document, is published in the{' '}
              <a
                href="https://github.com/We-Amp/pagespeed-optimizer/tree/main/sbom"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
              >
                <code>sbom/</code> directory
              </a>{' '}
              of the source tree. The apt and yum repositories are GPG-signed and every release
              asset is listed in <code>SHA256SUMS</code>. Every release from 2.6.0 on also carries
              its SPDX SBOM and VEX, for everyone. How to verify the signed packages is on the{' '}
              <a
                href="/support/#hardened-builds"
                class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
              >
                support page
              </a>
              .
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- Architecture note -->
      <div class="panel">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text-body">Architecture note</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          Both parts process untrusted content by design &mdash; they rewrite HTML, minify CSS and
          JavaScript, and transcode images from origins you configure. We take this attack surface
          seriously. Both parts of mod_pagespeed build on a current toolchain and run with
          AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer in CI, catching memory safety issues and
          undefined behavior before they reach production. The optimizer worker is written in C++23
          with smart pointers and RAII throughout.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- Threat model -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Threat model</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          mod_pagespeed processes untrusted images, CSS, JS, and HTML from origin servers. The
          module talks to the worker over a local Unix socket. The worker opens no outbound
          connection on its own: its network traffic is the origin fetches you configure and any key
          directories you enable, and its management API is off by default. All content parsing uses
          memory-safe patterns validated by continuous sanitizer testing (ASan, UBSan, TSan). No
          user data is transmitted externally. The full threat model is published in the
          mod_pagespeed repository as{' '}
          <a
            href="https://github.com/We-Amp/mod_pagespeed/blob/master/.oss-scanner/threat_model.md"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
          >
            <code>threat_model.md</code>
          </a>
          .
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- Supply chain -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Supply chain</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">
          All dependencies are pinned with SHA-256 checksums in the build configuration.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
